Detect the file type from its extension (CSV, TSV, XLS, XLSX, XLSM) and reject unsupported types immediately with a clear error.
Check the file isn't zero bytes before attempting to read it.
Detect the file's text encoding from a sample of raw bytes; flag a warning if detection confidence is low.
For CSV/TSV: detect the delimiter (comma, semicolon, tab, pipe); fall back to counting candidate delimiters in the first line if auto-detection fails; default to comma and flag "possible single-column file" if nothing is found.
Detect whether the first row is a header; default to assuming a header exists if detection fails.
For Excel: open the workbook, list all worksheets, use only the first one, and record which sheets were skipped.
Read the raw header row as plain text (before any library touches it) to catch true duplicate column names before they get silently renamed.
Load the file into a dataframe using the detected encoding/delimiter/header (or selected sheet); catch and report any load failure as a structured error instead of crashing.
Assign generic names to any missing/blank/unnamed header cells.
Clean up column names themselves — trim whitespace, normalize casing/spacing/special characters in the header row (not the data) — so column names are consistent and usable.
Reconcile and report any duplicate column names, including how the loader itself renamed them.
Check for a completely empty dataframe (zero columns) — stop and flag as fatal.
Check for a dataframe with zero rows — stop and flag as fatal.
Check for a very small row count (below a sensible minimum) — warn, don't stop.
Check for a single-column dataset — warn that correlation/multi-column analysis won't be possible.
Take a full snapshot of the raw, untouched data: per-column dtype, null count, unique count, and sample values. This copy is never modified again.
Strip leading/trailing whitespace from every text/string column.
Identify and separately count: actual nulls (NaN/None), empty strings, whitespace-only strings, and each null-like text marker ("na", "n/a", "null", "none", "missing", etc.) — per column, with a full breakdown, not just a total.
Roll these up into a dataset-level null/missingness summary (total missing, percentage, which columns are affected) — report these values, don't auto-fill or auto-delete them.
Detect and report exact duplicate rows across the whole dataset (count only, no removal yet).
Detect and report duplicate columns (two columns with identical values throughout) — flagged for review, not removed.
For every column, compute what percentage of non-null values would parse as numeric, as datetime, and as boolean (yes/no/true/false/y/n/1/0) — without committing to any conversion yet.
For every column, compute the uniqueness ratio (unique values ÷ row count) — needed to flag possible identifier columns.
For every column, compute the number of distinct values and, for string columns, the average string length — needed to distinguish categorical from free text.
Using all of the above evidence together, classify each column into one semantic type: numeric, datetime, boolean, categorical, identifier, free text, or unresolved.
Assign a confidence score to each column's classification, based on how strongly the evidence supports it.
Flag dataset-level warnings at this point: any fully-null column, any constant column (only one distinct value), any column with unexpectedly high cardinality relative to row count.
Check for mixed/inconsistent formats within a single column — e.g., dates in more than one format (DD/MM/YYYY vs MM/DD/YYYY), or numbers with mixed units (kg vs lbs, $ vs plain numbers) — and flag these for review rather than assuming one format.
For columns classified numeric with high confidence: convert to numeric type; check the null percentage before and after; if the conversion would destroy too much data, reject it and keep the column as-is, marked "unresolved — high data loss."
For columns classified datetime with high confidence: same approach as above — attempt conversion, measure data loss, accept or reject.
For columns classified boolean with high confidence: map recognized true/false representations to actual booleans; log any value that doesn't match a known pattern instead of silently discarding it.
For columns classified categorical: normalize text case (lower/upper/title) so equivalent categories aren't split apart (e.g. "usa" vs "USA").
For columns classified identifier: leave untouched, exclude from all statistics and visualizations, and log as "preserved."
For columns classified free text: leave untouched, exclude from numeric/statistical analysis, and log as "preserved."
For columns that remain unresolved or below the confidence threshold: do not guess — mark explicitly as "needs review" and leave untouched.
Guard every conversion step against fully empty or all-null columns — skip cleanly rather than process them as if they were normal.
Run outlier detection on every numeric column (e.g. IQR method) and report the count/values found — this is reported for analysis awareness, not automatically removed or corrected.
Compute skewness for every numeric column and flag columns that are heavily skewed, since this affects how the data should later be visualized or summarized.
Log every single action taken — column(s) affected, action name, status (executed/skipped/rejected/needs review), reason, and any before/after measurement (like data-loss percentage).
Separately distinguish and log "unsupported/unknown action" (a system issue) from "deliberately skipped" (a legitimate design decision).
Confirm the original raw dataset object is completely unmodified, by comparing it against the very first snapshot taken in step 16.
Re-profile the cleaned internal copy using the same profiling logic from steps 16–27.
Build a column-by-column diff comparing the raw profile against the cleaned profile: dtype before/after, null percentage before/after, unique count before/after, and which actions were applied.
Roll this up into a dataset-level before-and-after summary: total rows/columns before and after, number of columns changed, total number of logged actions.
Re-attach the original null/missingness summary (from step 19) into this final summary, so it stays visible even though it wasn't necessarily altered.
List every column still marked "unresolved" or "needs review," and exclude these from downstream statistics and visualizations.
State explicitly, in the final output, how many columns were excluded and the specific reason for each.
If the dataset is very large, apply row sampling only for the purpose of computing statistics/visualizations efficiently — this must not alter the actual cleaned dataset produced by this stage.
Package everything — ingestion details, structural report, raw profile, null summary, preprocessing plan, execution log, before-and-after summary, outlier/skew notes — into one structured result, ready to hand off to statistics and visualization, without exposing the cleaned dataset directly as a deliverable.